const BOOKS = [{"id": "understanding-mental-health", "title": "Understanding Mental Health", "author": "SpeakOut Mental Health Outreach", "category": "mental-health", "accessType": "free", "price": 0, "currency": "₦", "featured": true, "status": "active", "shortDescription": "A practical mental health literacy guide for young people, parents, teachers, ambassadors and community leaders.", "fullDescription": "Premium SpeakOut resource: A practical mental health literacy guide for young people, parents, teachers, ambassadors and community leaders.", "keyLessons": ["Understand the core topic.", "Apply practical tools.", "Complete reflection activities.", "Create an action plan."], "tags": ["mental-health", "premium", "speakout"], "readerHtml": "\n<div class=\"premium-book\">\n  <h1>Understanding Mental Health</h1>\n  <p><strong>SpeakOut Premium Learning Resource.</strong> A practical mental health literacy guide for young people, parents, teachers, ambassadors and community leaders.</p>\n  <div style=\"padding:18px;border-radius:18px;background:#eaf3ff;margin:20px 0;\">\n    <h2 style=\"margin-top:0;\">How to Use This Book</h2>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Read one chapter at a time.</li>\n      <li>Complete the activities before moving forward.</li>\n      <li>Use the reflection questions alone, in a club, classroom, family session or mentoring group.</li>\n      <li>End with the action plan and review it after seven days.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </div>\n  <h2>Learning Objectives</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand the subject clearly and practically.</li>\n    <li>Recognize common real-life challenges connected to the subject.</li>\n    <li>Practice safe, useful and measurable actions.</li>\n    <li>Build confidence to speak, learn, lead and seek support where necessary.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 1: Mental Health Is Part of Total Wellbeing</h2>\n    <p>Mental health affects how people think, feel, cope, relate with others and make decisions. It is not only about illness. It is also about daily emotional strength, support, self-awareness, healthy relationships and the ability to recover from pressure.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Everyone has mental health.</li><li>Mental health can improve or decline depending on life events, habits and support.</li><li>A person can look fine outside and still struggle inside.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student who used to enjoy school suddenly stops talking to friends, avoids class activities and becomes angry easily. Rather than labeling the child as stubborn, a supportive adult asks what has changed and offers help.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Write three signs that show someone is emotionally well and three signs that may show someone needs support.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What does mental health mean in your own words?</li><li>What affects your mood or confidence most?</li><li>What kind of support helps you feel safe?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Mental health is not shameful. It is part of being human.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 2: Emotions, Stress and Warning Signs</h2>\n    <p>Emotions are signals. Sadness, anger, fear, worry and frustration tell us something needs attention. A mental health concern may appear when distress becomes intense, lasts long or affects safety, sleep, learning, work or relationships.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Feelings are not bad, but unsafe actions must be corrected.</li><li>Stress can affect the body and behavior.</li><li>Early support prevents deeper crisis.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A young person preparing for exams feels headaches, cannot sleep and starts shouting at siblings. The issue may not be disrespect alone; it may be unmanaged stress.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Create a warning-sign map with three columns: body signs, thought signs and behavior signs.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What are your early warning signs?</li><li>What do you usually do when stressed?</li><li>Which response helps and which response harms?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Naming warning signs makes support easier.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 3: Stigma and Silence</h2>\n    <p>Stigma makes people hide emotional pain because they fear judgment. When people hear words like weak, mad, unserious or attention-seeking, they may stop asking for help. Safe language can create safe communities.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Stigma delays help-seeking.</li><li>Words can either open or close support.</li><li>Mental health education reduces fear.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>Instead of saying, 'Stop acting crazy,' a teacher can say, 'I can see you are overwhelmed. Let us talk calmly and find help.'</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Rewrite five harmful statements into supportive statements.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What mental health myths have you heard?</li><li>How can you correct stigma respectfully?</li><li>What should people avoid saying to someone in distress?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>The way we speak can protect dignity and save lives.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 4: Support and Help-Seeking</h2>\n    <p>Support can come from trusted adults, counsellors, health workers, teachers, parents, responsible relatives, faith leaders and trained professionals. In immediate danger, emergency help is required. No one should carry serious distress alone.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Support should be safe and confidential.</li><li>Professional help is not failure.</li><li>Emergency danger requires urgent response.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>If a young person says they want to harm themselves, it should not be treated as drama. A trusted adult should stay with them and seek urgent support immediately.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Build a support map with three trusted people and one professional support option.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>Who is in your support circle?</li><li>What makes someone safe to talk to?</li><li>What should you do if the first adult does not listen?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Seeking help is a responsible action.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <h2>Final Worksheet: My 7-Day Action Plan</h2>\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Area</th><th>Action I Will Take</th><th>When</th><th>Who Can Support Me?</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Mindset</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Habit</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Support</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <h2>Facilitator Note</h2>\n  <p>This book may be used in a school club, youth group, classroom, family session or ambassador training. The goal is not to rush completion; the goal is to help learners understand, reflect and apply.</p>\n</div>\n"}, {"id": "anxiety-and-stress-management", "title": "Anxiety & Stress Management", "author": "SpeakOut Mental Health Outreach", "category": "mental-health", "accessType": "free", "price": 0, "currency": "₦", "featured": true, "status": "active", "shortDescription": "A workbook for understanding pressure, calming the body and creating a personal stress plan.", "fullDescription": "Premium SpeakOut resource: A workbook for understanding pressure, calming the body and creating a personal stress plan.", "keyLessons": ["Understand the core topic.", "Apply practical tools.", "Complete reflection activities.", "Create an action plan."], "tags": ["mental-health", "premium", "speakout"], "readerHtml": "\n<div class=\"premium-book\">\n  <h1>Anxiety & Stress Management</h1>\n  <p><strong>SpeakOut Premium Learning Resource.</strong> A workbook for understanding pressure, calming the body and creating a personal stress plan.</p>\n  <div style=\"padding:18px;border-radius:18px;background:#eaf3ff;margin:20px 0;\">\n    <h2 style=\"margin-top:0;\">How to Use This Book</h2>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Read one chapter at a time.</li>\n      <li>Complete the activities before moving forward.</li>\n      <li>Use the reflection questions alone, in a club, classroom, family session or mentoring group.</li>\n      <li>End with the action plan and review it after seven days.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </div>\n  <h2>Learning Objectives</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand the subject clearly and practically.</li>\n    <li>Recognize common real-life challenges connected to the subject.</li>\n    <li>Practice safe, useful and measurable actions.</li>\n    <li>Build confidence to speak, learn, lead and seek support where necessary.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 1: Understanding Stress and Anxiety</h2>\n    <p>Stress is the body’s response to pressure. Anxiety is fear, worry or tension that may continue even when the danger is unclear. Both can affect concentration, sleep, appetite, mood and confidence.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Stress may be temporary or ongoing.</li><li>Anxiety can affect the mind and body.</li><li>Understanding triggers helps control reactions.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A learner may feel stomach pain before speaking in class. The body is reacting to fear of embarrassment.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>List your top five stress triggers and rate each from 1 to 5.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What situations make you anxious?</li><li>What do you avoid because of anxiety?</li><li>What pressure are you carrying now?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Anxiety becomes easier to manage when it is understood.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 2: Body Signals and Thought Patterns</h2>\n    <p>Anxiety can show as fast heartbeat, sweating, shaking, headaches, stomach upset, restlessness or racing thoughts. It may also create thoughts like 'I will fail', 'People will laugh', or 'I cannot handle this'.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>The body can sound an alarm even when danger is not present.</li><li>Thoughts are not always facts.</li><li>Calming the body helps the mind slow down.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>Before an interview, someone thinks, 'I will disgrace myself.' A better thought is, 'I am nervous, but I can prepare and answer one question at a time.'</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Write one anxious thought and replace it with a balanced thought.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What body signal appears first?</li><li>What thought repeats most?</li><li>What balanced thought can help?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>You can challenge anxious thoughts with truth and preparation.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 3: Calm-Down Tools</h2>\n    <p>Calm-down tools do not erase problems, but they help the body feel safe enough to think clearly. Useful tools include breathing, grounding, journaling, prayer, safe conversation, movement, rest and reducing overload.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Tools work better with practice.</li><li>Different people need different tools.</li><li>Unsafe coping should be replaced gradually.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student who wants to shout during conflict pauses, breathes slowly and asks for five minutes before responding.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Practice 4-count breathing: breathe in for 4, hold for 2, breathe out for 4. Repeat five times.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>Which calm tool works best for you?</li><li>What unhealthy reaction do you want to reduce?</li><li>Who can help you practice?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Calm is a skill, not a personality type.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 4: Personal Stress Plan</h2>\n    <p>A stress plan includes triggers, warning signs, coping tools, safe people and emergency steps. It helps you prepare before pressure becomes too high.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Planning reduces panic.</li><li>Support should be identified early.</li><li>Small steps can prevent bigger breakdowns.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student prepares for exam season by setting a reading timetable, sleeping earlier, reducing phone distractions and speaking to a mentor weekly.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Complete a stress plan: triggers, warning signs, helpful tools, support people and emergency action.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What is your first safe step when overwhelmed?</li><li>Who can you contact?</li><li>What should you avoid during high stress?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Preparation is protection.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <h2>Final Worksheet: My 7-Day Action Plan</h2>\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Area</th><th>Action I Will Take</th><th>When</th><th>Who Can Support Me?</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Mindset</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Habit</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Support</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <h2>Facilitator Note</h2>\n  <p>This book may be used in a school club, youth group, classroom, family session or ambassador training. The goal is not to rush completion; the goal is to help learners understand, reflect and apply.</p>\n</div>\n"}, {"id": "building-resilience", "title": "Building Resilience", "author": "SpeakOut Mental Health Outreach", "category": "mental-health", "accessType": "free", "price": 0, "currency": "₦", "featured": true, "status": "active", "shortDescription": "A recovery and confidence workbook for setbacks, disappointment, pressure and change.", "fullDescription": "Premium SpeakOut resource: A recovery and confidence workbook for setbacks, disappointment, pressure and change.", "keyLessons": ["Understand the core topic.", "Apply practical tools.", "Complete reflection activities.", "Create an action plan."], "tags": ["mental-health", "premium", "speakout"], "readerHtml": "\n<div class=\"premium-book\">\n  <h1>Building Resilience</h1>\n  <p><strong>SpeakOut Premium Learning Resource.</strong> A recovery and confidence workbook for setbacks, disappointment, pressure and change.</p>\n  <div style=\"padding:18px;border-radius:18px;background:#eaf3ff;margin:20px 0;\">\n    <h2 style=\"margin-top:0;\">How to Use This Book</h2>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Read one chapter at a time.</li>\n      <li>Complete the activities before moving forward.</li>\n      <li>Use the reflection questions alone, in a club, classroom, family session or mentoring group.</li>\n      <li>End with the action plan and review it after seven days.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </div>\n  <h2>Learning Objectives</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand the subject clearly and practically.</li>\n    <li>Recognize common real-life challenges connected to the subject.</li>\n    <li>Practice safe, useful and measurable actions.</li>\n    <li>Build confidence to speak, learn, lead and seek support where necessary.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 1: What Resilience Really Means</h2>\n    <p>Resilience is the ability to recover, adapt and keep growing after difficulty. It does not mean ignoring pain. It means responding with support, wisdom and courage.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Resilience is learned.</li><li>Recovery takes time.</li><li>Support strengthens resilience.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student fails an exam, feels embarrassed, but reviews mistakes, asks for help and prepares better for the next test.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Write about one challenge you survived and what helped you continue.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What does resilience mean to you?</li><li>Who helped you during a hard time?</li><li>What strength did you discover?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Resilience is built through supported action.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 2: Learning From Setbacks</h2>\n    <p>Failure and disappointment are painful, but they are not final identities. A setback can show what needs practice, rest, correction, support or a new strategy.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>A mistake is not your identity.</li><li>Reflection turns failure into learning.</li><li>Shame blocks growth.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A young leader organizes an event and attendance is low. Instead of quitting, they ask what affected turnout and improve publicity next time.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Complete: What happened? What did I learn? What will I try next?</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What setback still affects you?</li><li>What lesson can you take from it?</li><li>What would you tell a friend in the same situation?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>A setback can become a classroom.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 3: Support Systems and Safe People</h2>\n    <p>Resilient people are not people who never need help. They are people who know when to ask, who to ask and how to keep going with support.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Isolation increases pressure.</li><li>Safe people help recovery.</li><li>Support can be emotional, practical or professional.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A young person grieving a loss joins a supportive group, speaks with a trusted adult and slowly returns to routines.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Draw your resilience circle: family, friends, teachers, mentors, professionals and community support.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>Who belongs in your support circle?</li><li>Who encourages your growth?</li><li>What support do you need now?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Connection protects recovery.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 4: Confidence Through Small Action</h2>\n    <p>Confidence grows when people take small consistent steps. Waiting to feel ready can delay progress. Action, practice, feedback and rest build confidence over time.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Small steps matter.</li><li>Practice builds evidence.</li><li>Progress should be measured.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>Someone afraid of public speaking starts by speaking to one friend, then a small group, then a class.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Choose one small step you can complete within 24 hours.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What action can you take today?</li><li>What progress can you celebrate?</li><li>How will you keep going?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Confidence follows consistent practice.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <h2>Final Worksheet: My 7-Day Action Plan</h2>\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Area</th><th>Action I Will Take</th><th>When</th><th>Who Can Support Me?</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Mindset</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Habit</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Support</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <h2>Facilitator Note</h2>\n  <p>This book may be used in a school club, youth group, classroom, family session or ambassador training. The goal is not to rush completion; the goal is to help learners understand, reflect and apply.</p>\n</div>\n"}, {"id": "youth-leadership-handbook", "title": "Youth Leadership Handbook", "author": "SpeakOut Mental Health Outreach", "category": "leadership", "accessType": "free", "price": 0, "currency": "₦", "featured": true, "status": "active", "shortDescription": "A handbook for students, ambassadors and young changemakers who want to lead with service and integrity.", "fullDescription": "Premium SpeakOut resource: A handbook for students, ambassadors and young changemakers who want to lead with service and integrity.", "keyLessons": ["Understand the core topic.", "Apply practical tools.", "Complete reflection activities.", "Create an action plan."], "tags": ["leadership", "premium", "speakout"], "readerHtml": "\n<div class=\"premium-book\">\n  <h1>Youth Leadership Handbook</h1>\n  <p><strong>SpeakOut Premium Learning Resource.</strong> A handbook for students, ambassadors and young changemakers who want to lead with service and integrity.</p>\n  <div style=\"padding:18px;border-radius:18px;background:#eaf3ff;margin:20px 0;\">\n    <h2 style=\"margin-top:0;\">How to Use This Book</h2>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Read one chapter at a time.</li>\n      <li>Complete the activities before moving forward.</li>\n      <li>Use the reflection questions alone, in a club, classroom, family session or mentoring group.</li>\n      <li>End with the action plan and review it after seven days.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </div>\n  <h2>Learning Objectives</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand the subject clearly and practically.</li>\n    <li>Recognize common real-life challenges connected to the subject.</li>\n    <li>Practice safe, useful and measurable actions.</li>\n    <li>Build confidence to speak, learn, lead and seek support where necessary.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 1: Leadership as Service</h2>\n    <p>Leadership is not about title, popularity or control. It is the ability to influence others positively, solve problems and serve a purpose bigger than yourself.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Service comes before status.</li><li>Leadership starts with responsibility.</li><li>Influence must be ethical.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student leader notices classmates mocking another student and calmly redirects the group while reporting the bullying appropriately.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Identify one problem in your school or community that needs responsible leadership.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>How do you define leadership?</li><li>Who do you serve?</li><li>What problem can you help solve?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Real leadership improves others, not just the leader.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 2: Character and Integrity</h2>\n    <p>A leader’s character determines whether people can trust their influence. Integrity means doing what is right even when no one is watching.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Trust is earned.</li><li>Integrity protects influence.</li><li>Accountability strengthens leadership.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A club treasurer keeps accurate records and reports spending honestly even when no one is checking.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Write three leadership values you want to be known for.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What value matters most to you?</li><li>How do you respond when no one is watching?</li><li>What weak habit must you correct?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Character is the foundation of influence.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 3: Communication and Teamwork</h2>\n    <p>Leaders listen, explain, encourage and correct respectfully. Teams become stronger when people feel heard, valued and guided.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Listening is leadership.</li><li>Clear communication prevents confusion.</li><li>Correction should not humiliate.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>Instead of saying, 'You are useless,' a leader says, 'This task was missed. Let us agree on how to complete it today.'</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Practice a respectful correction sentence for a teammate who missed a task.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>Do you listen before responding?</li><li>How do you handle disagreement?</li><li>What makes a team feel safe?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>The best leaders make communication safe and clear.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 4: Leading Change</h2>\n    <p>Change begins with a clear problem, a simple plan, teamwork and consistent action. Young leaders do not need perfect conditions to start small.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Start small.</li><li>Measure progress.</li><li>Celebrate learning.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student group starts a weekly mental health discussion circle with teacher supervision and simple attendance tracking.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Create a one-week leadership action plan for one issue you care about.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What change do you want to lead?</li><li>Who should join you?</li><li>How will you know progress is happening?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Small organized action can create visible impact.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <h2>Final Worksheet: My 7-Day Action Plan</h2>\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Area</th><th>Action I Will Take</th><th>When</th><th>Who Can Support Me?</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Mindset</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Habit</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Support</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <h2>Facilitator Note</h2>\n  <p>This book may be used in a school club, youth group, classroom, family session or ambassador training. The goal is not to rush completion; the goal is to help learners understand, reflect and apply.</p>\n</div>\n"}, {"id": "career-planning-guide", "title": "Career Planning Guide", "author": "SpeakOut Mental Health Outreach", "category": "career", "accessType": "free", "price": 0, "currency": "₦", "featured": true, "status": "active", "shortDescription": "A practical workbook for students and young adults preparing for future opportunities.", "fullDescription": "Premium SpeakOut resource: A practical workbook for students and young adults preparing for future opportunities.", "keyLessons": ["Understand the core topic.", "Apply practical tools.", "Complete reflection activities.", "Create an action plan."], "tags": ["career", "premium", "speakout"], "readerHtml": "\n<div class=\"premium-book\">\n  <h1>Career Planning Guide</h1>\n  <p><strong>SpeakOut Premium Learning Resource.</strong> A practical workbook for students and young adults preparing for future opportunities.</p>\n  <div style=\"padding:18px;border-radius:18px;background:#eaf3ff;margin:20px 0;\">\n    <h2 style=\"margin-top:0;\">How to Use This Book</h2>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Read one chapter at a time.</li>\n      <li>Complete the activities before moving forward.</li>\n      <li>Use the reflection questions alone, in a club, classroom, family session or mentoring group.</li>\n      <li>End with the action plan and review it after seven days.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </div>\n  <h2>Learning Objectives</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand the subject clearly and practically.</li>\n    <li>Recognize common real-life challenges connected to the subject.</li>\n    <li>Practice safe, useful and measurable actions.</li>\n    <li>Build confidence to speak, learn, lead and seek support where necessary.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 1: Knowing Yourself</h2>\n    <p>Career planning begins with self-awareness. Your interests, strengths, values and personality can guide the kind of work and learning path that fits you.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Self-awareness guides choices.</li><li>Strengths can become opportunities.</li><li>Values affect satisfaction.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student who enjoys explaining things may explore teaching, training, communications, advocacy or content creation.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>List five strengths, five interests and three values that matter to you.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What do people ask you to help with?</li><li>What problems do you enjoy solving?</li><li>What environment helps you perform well?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>You cannot plan well if you do not understand yourself.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 2: Exploring Career Paths</h2>\n    <p>A career path is not always straight. It can include school, volunteering, internships, apprenticeships, business, digital skills and professional training.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>There are many pathways.</li><li>Skills can transfer across fields.</li><li>Exploration reduces confusion.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>Someone interested in healthcare may explore nursing, counselling, public health, health education or medical administration.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Research three careers and list required skills for each.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>Which career attracts you most?</li><li>What skill gap do you see?</li><li>Who can you ask for guidance?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Career clarity grows through exploration.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 3: Building Employable Skills</h2>\n    <p>Employers and communities value communication, reliability, problem-solving, digital skills, teamwork, writing, leadership and emotional maturity.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Skills require practice.</li><li>Soft skills matter.</li><li>Digital literacy increases opportunity.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A volunteer who keeps records, communicates updates and completes tasks on time is building work-ready evidence.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Choose one skill and create a 14-day practice plan.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>Which skill do you already have?</li><li>Which skill needs improvement?</li><li>How will you prove your skill?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>Skills become valuable when practiced and demonstrated.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <section style=\"margin-top:32px;\">\n    <h2>Chapter 4: Creating a Career Action Plan</h2>\n    <p>A career plan turns dreams into steps. It should include learning goals, mentors, practice, evidence of work and timelines.</p>\n    <h3>Key Concepts</h3>\n    <ul><li>Goals need timelines.</li><li>Evidence matters.</li><li>Mentorship speeds growth.</li></ul>\n    <h3>Real-Life Example</h3>\n    <p>A student creates a 30-day plan to learn Canva, design three samples and ask a mentor for feedback.</p>\n    <h3>Practice Activity</h3>\n    <p>Create a 30-day career action plan with three measurable goals.</p>\n    <h3>Reflection Questions</h3>\n    <ol><li>What will you do this week?</li><li>Who can review your progress?</li><li>What evidence will you build?</li></ol>\n    <h3>Takeaway</h3>\n    <p><strong>A dream becomes serious when it has a plan.</strong></p>\n  </section>\n\n  <h2>Final Worksheet: My 7-Day Action Plan</h2>\n  <table>\n    <tr><th>Area</th><th>Action I Will Take</th><th>When</th><th>Who Can Support Me?</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Mindset</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Habit</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Support</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <h2>Facilitator Note</h2>\n  <p>This book may be used in a school club, youth group, classroom, family session or ambassador training. The goal is not to rush completion; the goal is to help learners understand, reflect and apply.</p>\n</div>\n"}];
const KIDDIES = [{"id": "abc-adventures", "title": "ABC Adventures", "category": "literacy", "ageGroup": "ages-3-5", "featured": true, "status": "active", "description": "A premium early literacy mini-book for letters, sounds, picture words and confidence.", "contentHtml": "\n<h2>ABC Adventures</h2>\n<p><strong>SpeakOut Kiddies Premium Mini-Book.</strong> Children learn letters best when sounds, pictures and movement are connected.</p>\n<h3>Learning Goals</h3>\n<ul><li>Learn the main idea in simple words.</li><li>Practice through play, drawing, speaking or movement.</li><li>Build confidence and safe behavior.</li><li>Reflect with an adult or teacher.</li></ul>\n<h3>Meet the Letters</h3><p>Letters are symbols that help us read and write. Each letter has a name and often has a sound.</p><ul><li>A is for apple.</li><li>B is for ball.</li><li>C is for cup.</li><li>D is for dog.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Trace five letters in the air, say each letter name and make its sound.</p><h3>Sound Hunt</h3><p>A sound hunt helps children listen carefully and connect sounds to real objects.</p><ul><li>Say the sound slowly.</li><li>Find an object with that sound.</li><li>Repeat with praise.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Choose B and find ball, bag, book or banana.</p><h3>Picture Words</h3><p>Pictures help children build vocabulary before reading full sentences.</p><ul><li>Name the picture.</li><li>Say the first sound.</li><li>Clap the word parts.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Show five pictures and ask the child to name each one.</p>\n<h3>Child Reflection</h3><ol><li>What did I learn?</li><li>What did I enjoy?</li><li>What should I practice again?</li></ol>\n<h3>Parent / Teacher Guide</h3><p>Keep the session short, warm and practical. Praise effort. Do not shame mistakes. Repeat the lesson using real-life examples.</p>\n"}, {"id": "my-feelings", "title": "My Feelings", "category": "emotions", "ageGroup": "ages-6-9", "featured": true, "status": "active", "description": "A premium emotional wellbeing mini-book for naming feelings and choosing safe actions.", "contentHtml": "\n<h2>My Feelings</h2>\n<p><strong>SpeakOut Kiddies Premium Mini-Book.</strong> Children need language for emotions so they can express feelings safely.</p>\n<h3>Learning Goals</h3>\n<ul><li>Learn the main idea in simple words.</li><li>Practice through play, drawing, speaking or movement.</li><li>Build confidence and safe behavior.</li><li>Reflect with an adult or teacher.</li></ul>\n<h3>Feelings Are Messages</h3><p>Feelings tell us something is happening inside. They are not bad, but our actions must be safe.</p><ul><li>Happy</li><li>Sad</li><li>Angry</li><li>Scared</li><li>Worried</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Draw five faces and name each feeling.</p><h3>Big Feelings</h3><p>Sometimes feelings become too strong. Children can pause, breathe and ask for help.</p><ul><li>Stop.</li><li>Breathe.</li><li>Name the feeling.</li><li>Ask for help.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Practice: I feel ____ because ____. I need ____.</p><h3>Trusted Adults</h3><p>Children should know who they can talk to when feelings are too big.</p><ul><li>Parent or caregiver</li><li>Teacher</li><li>Counsellor</li><li>Responsible relative</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Draw a hand and write one trusted adult on each finger.</p>\n<h3>Child Reflection</h3><ol><li>What did I learn?</li><li>What did I enjoy?</li><li>What should I practice again?</li></ol>\n<h3>Parent / Teacher Guide</h3><p>Keep the session short, warm and practical. Praise effort. Do not shame mistakes. Repeat the lesson using real-life examples.</p>\n"}, {"id": "safety-first", "title": "Safety First", "category": "safety", "ageGroup": "ages-6-9", "featured": true, "status": "active", "description": "A premium safety mini-book about body safety, boundaries and trusted adults.", "contentHtml": "\n<h2>Safety First</h2>\n<p><strong>SpeakOut Kiddies Premium Mini-Book.</strong> Children need clear, calm safety education that teaches boundaries without creating fear.</p>\n<h3>Learning Goals</h3>\n<ul><li>Learn the main idea in simple words.</li><li>Practice through play, drawing, speaking or movement.</li><li>Build confidence and safe behavior.</li><li>Reflect with an adult or teacher.</li></ul>\n<h3>My Body Belongs to Me</h3><p>Every child’s body deserves respect and protection.</p><ul><li>I can say no to unsafe touch.</li><li>I can move away.</li><li>I can tell a trusted adult.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Practice saying: Stop. I do not like that. I need help.</p><h3>Safe and Unsafe Secrets</h3><p>Safe surprises make people happy later. Unsafe secrets make children afraid or confused.</p><ul><li>Birthday surprises can be safe.</li><li>Touch secrets are unsafe.</li><li>Threats are unsafe.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Ask: safe surprise or unsafe secret? Use examples.</p><h3>Safety Circle</h3><p>A safety circle is a group of trusted adults who listen and protect.</p><ul><li>Tell one trusted adult.</li><li>Tell another if needed.</li><li>Keep speaking until someone helps.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Draw a circle and write three trusted adults inside it.</p>\n<h3>Child Reflection</h3><ol><li>What did I learn?</li><li>What did I enjoy?</li><li>What should I practice again?</li></ol>\n<h3>Parent / Teacher Guide</h3><p>Keep the session short, warm and practical. Praise effort. Do not shame mistakes. Repeat the lesson using real-life examples.</p>\n"}, {"id": "healthy-habits", "title": "Healthy Habits", "category": "wellness", "ageGroup": "ages-6-9", "featured": true, "status": "active", "description": "A premium wellbeing mini-book for hygiene, movement, rest, water and screen balance.", "contentHtml": "\n<h2>Healthy Habits</h2>\n<p><strong>SpeakOut Kiddies Premium Mini-Book.</strong> Small daily habits help children learn, play and manage feelings better.</p>\n<h3>Learning Goals</h3>\n<ul><li>Learn the main idea in simple words.</li><li>Practice through play, drawing, speaking or movement.</li><li>Build confidence and safe behavior.</li><li>Reflect with an adult or teacher.</li></ul>\n<h3>My Body Needs Care</h3><p>A healthy body supports energy, learning and mood.</p><ul><li>Drink water.</li><li>Wash hands.</li><li>Brush teeth.</li><li>Sleep well.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Make a daily care checklist and tick it each day.</p><h3>Move and Rest</h3><p>Children need both active play and quiet rest.</p><ul><li>Play safely.</li><li>Stretch.</li><li>Rest when tired.</li><li>Sleep early.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Do ten jumps, five stretches and one minute of calm breathing.</p><h3>Screen Balance</h3><p>Screens can be useful, but too much screen time can affect sleep and attention.</p><ul><li>Take breaks.</li><li>Avoid screens before sleep.</li><li>Choose learning content.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Pick one screen-free activity today.</p>\n<h3>Child Reflection</h3><ol><li>What did I learn?</li><li>What did I enjoy?</li><li>What should I practice again?</li></ol>\n<h3>Parent / Teacher Guide</h3><p>Keep the session short, warm and practical. Praise effort. Do not shame mistakes. Repeat the lesson using real-life examples.</p>\n"}, {"id": "leadership-for-kids", "title": "Leadership for Kids", "category": "leadership", "ageGroup": "ages-10-13", "featured": true, "status": "active", "description": "A premium leadership mini-book for service, teamwork, courage and responsibility.", "contentHtml": "\n<h2>Leadership for Kids</h2>\n<p><strong>SpeakOut Kiddies Premium Mini-Book.</strong> Children can learn leadership as service, kindness and responsibility.</p>\n<h3>Learning Goals</h3>\n<ul><li>Learn the main idea in simple words.</li><li>Practice through play, drawing, speaking or movement.</li><li>Build confidence and safe behavior.</li><li>Reflect with an adult or teacher.</li></ul>\n<h3>A Leader Helps</h3><p>Leadership is not bossing people. A good leader helps and does what is right.</p><ul><li>Be honest.</li><li>Be kind.</li><li>Take responsibility.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Write one helpful action you can do this week.</p><h3>Teamwork</h3><p>Teams work better when everyone feels respected.</p><ul><li>Listen.</li><li>Share ideas.</li><li>Take turns.</li><li>Solve problems together.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Build something with a group and give everyone a role.</p><h3>Courage</h3><p>A leader may feel nervous but still chooses the right action.</p><ul><li>Speak respectfully.</li><li>Report unsafe behavior.</li><li>Try again after mistakes.</li></ul><h4>Activity</h4><p>Practice a one-minute speech on why kindness matters.</p>\n<h3>Child Reflection</h3><ol><li>What did I learn?</li><li>What did I enjoy?</li><li>What should I practice again?</li></ol>\n<h3>Parent / Teacher Guide</h3><p>Keep the session short, warm and practical. Praise effort. Do not shame mistakes. Repeat the lesson using real-life examples.</p>\n"}];
